@page "/"

<PageTitle>DataArc Orchestration Demo</PageTitle>

<div class="container-fluid px-0">

    <section class="mb-5">
        <div class="p-4 p-lg-5 rounded-3 border bg-body-tertiary">
            <div class="row align-items-center g-4">

                <div class="col-lg-8">
                    <h1 class="display-4 fw-semibold mb-3">
                        Explicit Application Orchestration
                    </h1>

                    <p class="lead mb-3">
                        Use-case-oriented orchestration for modular .NET applications.
                    </p>

                    <p class="mb-0">
                        This demo shows how DataArc keeps business policy,
                        application coordination, observability and persistence
                        mechanics separate while coordinating work across multiple
                        EF Core <code>DbContext</code> boundaries.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="col-lg-4">
                    <div class="card shadow-sm h-100">
                        <div class="card-body">
                            <h2 class="h5">Core idea</h2>

                            <p class="mb-0">
                                Give cross-boundary execution mechanics a dedicated
                                home without moving business rules out of the domain.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

            </div>
        </div>
    </section>


    <section class="mb-5">
        <h2 class="mb-3">
            Why a dedicated orchestration layer?
        </h2>

        <p class="mb-4">
            The goal is not to remove architectural boundaries.
            It is to avoid creating application plumbing merely because a pattern
            says another abstraction must exist.
        </p>

        <div class="row g-4">

            <div class="col-md-6 col-xl-3">
                <div class="card h-100 shadow-sm">
                    <div class="card-body">
                        <h3 class="h5">
                            Repository Proliferation
                        </h3>

                        <p class="mb-0">
                            Avoid repositories for repository's sake and repository
                            permutations created only to coordinate different
                            persistence boundaries.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="col-md-6 col-xl-3">
                <div class="card h-100 shadow-sm">
                    <div class="card-body">
                        <h3 class="h5">
                            Command / Query Sprawl
                        </h3>

                        <p class="mb-0">
                            Queries and commands that belong to one coherent use case
                            can remain together instead of becoming separate handlers
                            for every persistence or column permutation.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="col-md-6 col-xl-3">
                <div class="card h-100 shadow-sm">
                    <div class="card-body">
                        <h3 class="h5">
                            Handler Sprawl
                        </h3>

                        <p class="mb-0">
                            Keep meaningful domain events without turning every
                            application step into another command handler,
                            query handler or event handler.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="col-md-6 col-xl-3">
                <div class="card h-100 shadow-sm">
                    <div class="card-body">
                        <h3 class="h5">
                            Use-Case Cohesion
                        </h3>

                        <p class="mb-0">
                            An explicit orchestrator can query what the use case needs,
                            execute its persistence operations and coordinate the
                            participating boundaries in one place.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

        </div>
    </section>


    <section class="mb-5">
        <h2 class="mb-3">
            The architectural flow
        </h2>

        <div class="card shadow-sm">
            <div class="card-body p-4">

                <div class="row g-4 text-center">

                    <div class="col-md-6 col-xl">
                        <div class="h5 mb-2">Application</div>
                        <div class="text-body-secondary small">
                            Coordinates the use case
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="col-md-6 col-xl">
                        <div class="h5 mb-2">Domain Policy</div>
                        <div class="text-body-secondary small">
                            Decides whether the operation is allowed
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="col-md-6 col-xl">
                        <div class="h5 mb-2">Port</div>
                        <div class="text-body-secondary small">
                            Decouples the application from execution
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="col-md-6 col-xl">
                        <div class="h5 mb-2">Orchestration</div>
                        <div class="text-body-secondary small">
                            Coordinates persistence
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="col-md-6 col-xl">
                        <div class="h5 mb-2">Persistence</div>
                        <div class="text-body-secondary small">
                            Stores state
                        </div>
                    </div>

                </div>

                <hr class="my-4" />

                <p class="mb-0 text-center">
                    <strong>
                        Business rules remain in the domain.
                    </strong>
                    The orchestration layer executes an already-approved
                    persistence workflow.
                </p>

            </div>
        </div>
    </section>


    <section class="mb-5">
        <h2 class="mb-3">
            Two kinds of orchestration
        </h2>

        <div class="row g-4">

            <div class="col-lg-6">
                <div class="card h-100 shadow-sm">
                    <div class="card-body">

                        <h3 class="h4">
                            .NET Aspire
                        </h3>

                        <p>
                            Aspire orchestrates the applications and development
                            environment.
                        </p>

                        <ul class="mb-0">
                            <li>Application hosting</li>
                            <li>Service discovery</li>
                            <li>Health checks</li>
                            <li>Logs</li>
                            <li>Metrics</li>
                            <li>Distributed tracing</li>
                        </ul>

                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="col-lg-6">
                <div class="card h-100 shadow-sm">
                    <div class="card-body">

                        <h3 class="h4">
                            DataArc
                        </h3>

                        <p>
                            DataArc orchestrates explicit application work inside
                            the application process.
                        </p>

                        <ul class="mb-0">
                            <li>Use-case orchestration</li>
                            <li>Domain-policy integration</li>
                            <li>Observer event dispatch</li>
                            <li>Cross-module coordination</li>
                            <li>Multi-DbContext persistence</li>
                            <li>Transactional execution</li>
                        </ul>

                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

        </div>
    </section>


    <section class="mb-5">
        <h2 class="mb-3">
            Solution architecture
        </h2>

        <div class="card shadow-sm">
            <div class="card-body">

                <img src="@Assets["images/Architecture.png"]"
                     alt="DataArc orchestration with clean architecture"
                     class="img-fluid" />

            </div>
        </div>
    </section>


    <section class="mb-5">
        <h2 class="mb-3">
            Application boundaries
        </h2>

        <div class="row g-4">

            <div class="col-md-6 col-xl-3">
                <div class="card h-100 shadow-sm">
                    <div class="card-body">

                        <h3 class="h5">
                            Presentation
                        </h3>

                        <p class="mb-0">
                            Razor Components and ASP.NET Core endpoints expose
                            application outcomes without owning business policy or
                            persistence coordination.
                        </p>

                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="col-md-6 col-xl-3">
                <div class="card h-100 shadow-sm">
                    <div class="card-body">

                        <h3 class="h5">
                            Application + Domain
                        </h3>

                        <p class="mb-0">
                            Feature services coordinate the use case while rich
                            domain values and policies own the business decision.
                        </p>

                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="col-md-6 col-xl-3">
                <div class="card h-100 shadow-sm">
                    <div class="card-body">

                        <h3 class="h5">
                            Orchestration
                        </h3>

                        <p class="mb-0">
                            Ports, adapters and orchestrators isolate the application
                            from transaction handling, save order, connections and
                            cross-context persistence mechanics.
                        </p>

                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="col-md-6 col-xl-3">
                <div class="card h-100 shadow-sm">
                    <div class="card-body">

                        <h3 class="h5">
                            Persistence
                        </h3>

                        <p class="mb-0">
                            Multiple EF Core contexts preserve application
                            boundaries while participating in one relational
                            SQL Server model.
                        </p>

                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

        </div>
    </section>


    <section class="mb-5">
        <h2 class="mb-3">
            Policy decisions are observable
        </h2>

        <div class="row g-4">

            <div class="col-lg-7">
                <div class="card h-100 shadow-sm">
                    <div class="card-body">

                        <h3 class="h5">
                            One decision, multiple consumers
                        </h3>

                        <p>
                            The onboarding policy produces both the business outcome
                            and domain events describing that outcome.
                        </p>

                        <pre class="mb-3"><code>await _observableEventHandler.DispatchAsync(
    policyResult.DomainEvents);</code></pre>

                        <p class="mb-0">
                            The caller receives meaningful business information while
                            observers independently expose the same decision through
                            logs, traces and metrics.
                        </p>

                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="col-lg-5">
                <div class="card h-100 shadow-sm">
                    <div class="card-body">

                        <h3 class="h5">
                            Observer pipeline
                        </h3>

                        <ul class="mb-0">
                            <li>Structured logging</li>
                            <li>Activity tracing</li>
                            <li>Metrics</li>
                            <li>Diagnostics</li>
                            <li>Auditing or future integrations</li>
                        </ul>

                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

        </div>
    </section>


    <section class="mb-5">
        <h2 class="mb-3">
            Business rejection is not a system failure
        </h2>

        <div class="row g-4">

            <div class="col-lg-4">
                <div class="card h-100 shadow-sm">
                    <div class="card-body">

                        <h3 class="h5">
                            Validation
                        </h3>

                        <p class="mb-0">
                            The request itself is malformed or incomplete.
                        </p>

                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="col-lg-4">
                <div class="card h-100 shadow-sm">
                    <div class="card-body">

                        <h3 class="h5">
                            Policy Rejection
                        </h3>

                        <p class="mb-0">
                            The request is understood, but a business rule says the
                            operation must not proceed. The UI receives the policy
                            reason and observers record the same decision.
                        </p>

                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="col-lg-4">
                <div class="card h-100 shadow-sm">
                    <div class="card-body">

                        <h3 class="h5">
                            System Failure
                        </h3>

                        <p class="mb-0">
                            Persistence, infrastructure and unexpected technical
                            failures roll back execution and propagate as exceptions
                            rather than masquerading as business outcomes.
                        </p>

                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

        </div>
    </section>


    <section class="mb-5">
        <h2 class="mb-3">
            Composed relational model
        </h2>

        <p>
            ASP.NET Core Identity and four business contexts participate in one
            physical relational database while preserving explicit application
            boundaries and database-enforced relationships.
        </p>

        <div class="card shadow-sm">
            <div class="card-body">

                <img src="@Assets["images/ERD.png"]"
                     alt="DataArc Orchestration Demo database model"
                     class="img-fluid" />

            </div>
        </div>
    </section>


    <section class="mb-5">
        <h2 class="mb-3">
            What this demo demonstrates
        </h2>

        <div class="row g-4">

            <div class="col-md-6 col-xl-3">
                <div class="card h-100 shadow-sm">
                    <div class="card-body">

                        <h3 class="h5">
                            Modular DbContexts
                        </h3>

                        <p class="mb-0">
                            Identity, HR, Finance, IT and Operations remain
                            separate application persistence boundaries.
                        </p>

                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="col-md-6 col-xl-3">
                <div class="card h-100 shadow-sm">
                    <div class="card-body">

                        <h3 class="h5">
                            Domain Policy
                        </h3>

                        <p class="mb-0">
                            Business rules decide whether onboarding may proceed
                            before persistence execution begins.
                        </p>

                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="col-md-6 col-xl-3">
                <div class="card h-100 shadow-sm">
                    <div class="card-body">

                        <h3 class="h5">
                            Observable Decisions
                        </h3>

                        <p class="mb-0">
                            Accepted and rejected policy decisions flow through
                            DataArc.Observer into logs, traces and metrics.
                        </p>

                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="col-md-6 col-xl-3">
                <div class="card h-100 shadow-sm">
                    <div class="card-body">

                        <h3 class="h5">
                            Transactional Orchestration
                        </h3>

                        <p class="mb-0">
                            HR, Finance, IT and Operations persistence is coordinated
                            by one explicit onboarding workflow and one local
                            transaction.
                        </p>

                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

        </div>
    </section>


    <section class="mb-5">
        <h2 class="mb-3">
            SQL Server database composition
        </h2>

        <div class="card shadow-sm">
            <div class="card-body">

                <p>
                    The integration-test path also demonstrates the commercial
                    <code>DataArc.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer</code> DDL Builder.
                </p>

                <p class="mb-0">
                    Five EF Core contexts are composed into the physical demo
                    database, SQL scripts are generated, and the complete
                    onboarding workflow is executed against a real SQL Server
                    database.
                </p>

            </div>
        </div>
    </section>


    <section class="pb-4">
        <div class="border-top pt-4 d-flex flex-column flex-lg-row justify-content-between gap-3">

            <div>
                <strong>
                    Solid Arc Software
                </strong>

                <div class="text-body-secondary small">
                    Strategize. Observe. Adapt. Provide.
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="text-body-secondary small align-self-lg-end">
                Domain policy. Observable decisions. Explicit orchestration.
            </div>

        </div>
    </section>

</div>